    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I fixed `mock_openai()` in `tests/fakes.py`, and both digest tests pass again. I didn't touch `app/`, the pytest configuration or any packages.\n\n**Cause:** openai 3.19.2 no longer sends requests through `httpx`. It uses a separate package, `httpx2`, with its own `httpx2.Client`. respx only patches `httpx`, so its routes never saw these requests. Nothing warned because respx only complains about requests it sees and doesn't handle.\n\n**Fix:** for the length of the `with` block, the helper temporarily replaces `httpx2.Client._transport_for_url`, the method every `httpx2.Client` calls to pick a transport for each request. It now returns an `httpx2.MockTransport` that gives the canned replies. Because the patch is on the class, it covers app.digest's cached client, clients created before the block and clients created inside it. The patch is undone when the block exits.\n- Behaviour is unchanged otherwise: the helper's interface stays the same, it yields the request bodies it saw, and a request beyond the canned replies still gets a 400.\n- As respx did before, any request other than `POST \u2026/chat/completions` fails with an error instead of reaching the network.\n- I kept the respx router too, so an old-style `httpx.Client` passed in as `http_client` is still caught.\n\nI didn't use `httpx2.alias_httpx()`. It only works if it runs before anything imports `httpx`, and it affects the whole process, so it would have needed a pytest configuration change.\n\n**Checked:** I added a temporary test file, `tests/test_tmp_check.py`. It confirmed that inside the block, a client created before it, a client created inside it and `digest.summarize` all got the canned replies in order, and an extra request got the 400. After the block, both the earlier client and `digest.summarize` tried to reach the network again, and the test setup blocked them. All of that passed.\n\n**You need to delete `tests/test_tmp_check.py`:** removing it needed an approval that wasn't given, so it's still there. With it"
  },
